00:03:36.333 --> 00:03:40.344
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:41.183 --> 00:03:47.681
Visal, Dr. Sotheara is now the leading authority on tropical diseases in Cambodia. How did she get there?

58
00:03:48.783 --> 00:03:56.286
Having spent fifteen years in rural clinics, she understood problems that laboratory researchers had overlooked.

59
00:03:57.383 --> 00:03:59.162
Was experience alone enough?

60
00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:10.019
No. Having combined practice with rigorous research, she published findings that changed national treatment guidelines.

61
00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:12.025
And now?

62
00:04:13.117 --> 00:04:22.721
Having achieved recognition, she devotes much of her time to mentorship, because mastery that isn't passed on dies with its owner.

63
00:04:23.867 --> 00:04:25.471
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:26.317 --> 00:04:29.442
Grammar focus: Participial phrases.

65
00:04:29.742 --> 00:04:30.929
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:31.617 --> 00:04:35.485
Having built real expertise, she became an industry authority.

67
00:04:36.383 --> 00:04:40.600
Having spent years in rural clinics, she understood overlooked problems.

68
00:04:41.517 --> 00:04:45.374
Having achieved recognition, she now focuses on mentorship.

69
00:04:46.333 --> 00:04:59.324
The perfect participle ('Having + p.p.') shows an action completed before the main action, often implying cause. The subject must match: 'Having finished the report, she left' — not 'Having finished the report, the office closed.'

70
00:05:00.083 --> 00:05:04.024
Be careful! Don't say: Having finished, the report was sent.

71
00:05:05.967 --> 00:05:08.616
Say: Having finished, she sent the report.

72
00:05:08.916 --> 00:05:11.427
Participle subject = main subject.

73
00:05:12.233 --> 00:05:14.781
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:17.508
Beyond Ten Thousand Hours.

75
00:05:17.808 --> 00:05:25.473
Popular accounts often reduce mastery to a simple formula, claiming that ten thousand hours of practice will produce expertise in any field.

76
00:05:26.467 --> 00:05:35.966
Research by Anders Ericsson, however, emphasised the quality of practice rather than its quantity, highlighting focused effort, expert feedback and continual stretching beyond comfort.

77
00:05:36.967 --> 00:05:43.900
Having reached a high level of skill, true masters typically shift their attention from personal performance to shaping their profession.

78
00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:51.172
They become thought leaders, mentors and guardians of standards, ensuring that knowledge survives beyond a single career.

79
00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:58.682
Mastery, in this sense, is measured not only by what professionals can do, but by what they enable others to do.

80
